hi there Steve Kaufman here today I'm  going to talk about the importance of  context in language learning and I  haven't as is usually the case usually  the case with these I don't have the  written out in advance so my thoughts  are gonna be a little bit disjointed  bear with me here but in language  learning yeah let's start with what sort  of triggered this thought of my mind I  met this morning with a family again  Chinese immigrants father and mother and  their daughter they've been here for 12  years the daughter speaks fluent English  of course has been here since the age of  four she's 16 and both parents struggle  with their English and the the mother  there said yes you know I find it very  difficult when I go to study a list of  words I can't seem to remember them and  it's very frustrating she's been in a  drought years she really can't speak  English very well at all and I thought  to myself well I can't learn from a list  of words because a list of words has no  meaning for me it has no resonance  resonance there's nothing there for me  to grab on to both the parents have  lived here for 12 years don't speak  English very well obviously English is  not very important to them so that  that's another context that's not there  they don't have a strong sense of  wanting to participate in an  english-speaking society so that that  there isn't that context of wanting to  participate in the language but context  goes beyond that it goes you have in my  experience if I learn from you know some  content has resonance for me it's  interesting to me when I'm listening to  some of my Czech material about the  history of Czechoslovakia or whatever it  might be that's of interest to me other  people are interested in other things it  might be music it might be whatever but  but whatever you're learning from has to  be relevant has to be meaningful has to  have resonance it has to have  credibility this is another problem very  often if we're learning from the typical  sort of beginner texts we're not  entirely sure that that's how people  speak at least I'm not I'm not entirely  sure that this is authentic and credible  because in fact it's scripted for me so  you know and I know that some of the  words that they're teaching are not very  important you know most beginner texts  have you going through customs I wonder  how you know how many people have ever  used the language they're learning while  going through customs I know I haven't  so that you start to question the  relevance of this context it's low  resonance obviously reading a list of  words has very little resonance  resonance I think it's so important that  we get engaged you know emotionally with  with the language and the context that  we're learning from and that context is  not only the subject matter that we  might be reading or listening to it's  also the people we associate with it's  our desire to be part of that community  that gives resonance that create a  meaningful context makes it real  credible authentic so yeah I think one  of the difficulties very often that  immigrants from Asia have is that they  seem to be less willing to inject  themselves into the local scene so  therefore English becomes real less  relevant to them it's it's it's not a  meaningful context for them so I don't  know if this makes sense but I think  it's extremely important because you  know the brain is not just some kind of  a machine that you throw them some stuff  in and turn up a handle and outcomes  language ability it's not just the  so-called left side of your brain which  is good at you know dealing with  rational explanations or whatever I  think that there's ample evidence that  it's the whole of the brain that gets  involved and that emotion is a big part  of it and if the learning environment or  the living environment or the  learning materials are not if a context  in which we are learning in every sense  of the word if that context context is  not rich is not authentic credible  vibrant that doesn't grab us then we'll  have a lot tougher time learning so  that's really all I wanted to say I  don't know what we can do about it other  than trying to find or create meaningful  context in other words learning from  material that we like and find  interesting where the voice has a  resonance where the subject matter has  residents a resonance I can still  remember a text that I listened to an  Italian you know promesa Sposi or Anna  Karenina and Russian or some of the  material I'm listening to now in Czech  and and and so that that creates an  attachment and that's gonna help you  learn so and also I think in terms of  interacting with the language as you  know my preference is to wait until  until I have enough of a vocabulary that  I can actually have a meaningful again  interaction one that's authentic and  real and we're actually communicating  meaning so that I'm driven by the desire  to communicate my meaning and understand  the meaning of the other person  I'm not displaying my ability to use the  subjunctive or worrying about whether I  say everything correctly or not because  I've got a meaningful context and it's  meaningful because I have enough of a  vocabulary that I can actually have a  meaningful conversation and have a  chance of understanding what the other  person is saying so that makes that  whole context meaningful so you know do  with this idea what you want but I think  those people who are able to create  meaningful rich authentic credible  learning contexts are generally more  successful language learning language  learners so I look forward to your  comments thank you for this thing bye  for now
 

